WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.970
This is a special video.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:06.610
Today we are talking about a topic the heavily affects this channel and, in general, all

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.889
of YouTube as a platform.

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:13.169
Don’t worry though, nobody is going to shut
down this channel BUT, maybe those who watch

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:16.990
us from the European Union will no longer
be able to watch some of our videos.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.630
Yes, dear viewer, today we’re talking about
ARTICLE 13.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.530
Something you might of heard of, especially
if you live in Europe because YouTube seems

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:27.320
to have replaced all of my ads which before
were “Would you like a chocolate bar!!”

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:34.820
to be “Have you heard about ARTICLE 13?!”

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.610
Now let’s quickly make one thing clear.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:38.610
We’re biased.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.060
(Not a huge surpise though)

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.649
In fact, we are a playing an active role in
this matter.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:50.699
One member of the VisualPolitik team was in
Brussels talking about Article 13 with policy

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.340
makers who are currently negotiating it.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.859
That would be Fonseca (ALSO known as “drunk
Spanish guy doing the title voiceovers in

00:00:57.859 --> 00:00:59.030
some of our earlier episodes”).

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:04.590
FUNNY HEAR TO ADD A CLIP.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:09.260
To this day, there is no association representing
European online creators.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.400
This is why a group of European YouTube channels
decided to take a bit of initiative and show

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.330
our concerns to the relevant politicians.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:17.820
VisualPolitik were are one of them.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:24.570
So, for those who don’t know it, Article
13 is part of a EU directive to regulate copyright

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.570
online.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.130
But the question now is…

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.010
What exactly is article 13?

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.610
Is it as terrible as many people say?

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:35.850
And the most important question of all is:
what can we, the content creators, do about

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.850
it?

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.070
Today we are going to answer to all of these
questions but, before we do… let’s take

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:50.290
a look back at history.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:53.220
For the last few years answering the “What
do you do for a living?”

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.312
question got complicated for me.

00:01:55.312 --> 00:01:59.440
Some people, especially maybe the older set,
tend to think “well, that’s nice dear,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.630
but what about money?”

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.530
Some of them I just gave up, and I’m pretty
sure my grandma thinks I’m a television

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:11.519
presenter here in Prague.

00:02:11.519 --> 00:02:20.129
But the answer is that, yes, I make a living
doing this probably not suprising to all of

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:21.379
you millenials watching…

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:24.239
Now we Britsh we don’t like talking about
money.

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:29.069
Indeed, fun fact, we’re seven times more
likely to tell someone we had an STD than

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:30.749
we are to reveal our income.

00:02:30.749 --> 00:02:36.349
But, awkwardness aside, lets just take VisualPolitik,
both the English and the Spanish.

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:39.620
Combined they get about 10 million views a
month.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:44.060
As you can imagine, this translates into something,
it’s a lot of people watching...

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.569
Now, no, VisualPolitik doesn’t make us millionaires.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:52.220
But, along with the Patreon donations (thank
you!) and the ads, we can all draw a decent

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:53.220
salary.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.379
And look… we are not the only ones!

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:59.629
There are thousands of creators just like
us all across Europe: creators with enough

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:06.999
audience to make an income and even, create
new jobs.

00:03:06.999 --> 00:03:08.700
And that’s just YouTube.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.290
It also happens on other platforms like Instagram,
Facebook, and Twitch.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.299
This means online creators have become an
INDUSTRY.

00:03:14.299 --> 00:03:18.219
Alright… so it’s not exactly the oil and
gas industry, but it does exist.

00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:23.489
It’s an industry that employs thousands
of people in Europe and creates value to society

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:24.930
(most of the time…).

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.109
Yes, YouTube might be an American company.

00:03:27.109 --> 00:03:33.310
But the World’s biggest YouTuber is PewDewPie
and he is Swedish.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.859
Is he still the biggest?

00:03:35.859 --> 00:03:36.859
HE IS.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:37.859
Still beating T-Series.

00:03:37.859 --> 00:03:38.859
Go PewDiePie go.

00:03:38.859 --> 00:03:39.859
Alright.

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:41.930
so what’s the problem, then?

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:50.090
Well, like I said, there is no association
that represents us creators in Europe.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:55.379
Media corporations or record labels have already
trade associations and even big lobbies in

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:56.639
Brussels.

00:03:56.639 --> 00:03:57.639
We don’t.

00:03:57.639 --> 00:03:58.700
And why?

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.730
Mainly because we started little by little.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:06.760
The story behind any YouTuber is: you start
small, make a few videos, hopefully see a

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.919
bit of growth and then you say ‘OK, now
I can go full time on this’.

00:04:10.919 --> 00:04:20.430
As you can imagine, during this process, nobody
thinks about creating a trade association...

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:24.539
Ya, know just in case, one day, the European
Union is like “hmm lets introduce a big

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:29.900
old directive that really affects these people”.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:40.190
OK, at this point its probably important for
you to know how copyright actually works in

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:41.190
Europe?

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.180
Well, to this day, we are under the so called,
“Ecommerce Directive” and, also, the “Copyright

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.180
directive”.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.009
Both were passed back in 2001.

00:04:49.009 --> 00:04:51.379
So how does this legislation work?

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:57.789
OK, let’s imagine I upload to VisualPolitik
a videoclip from a band.

00:04:57.789 --> 00:05:02.349
For example, let’s say I upload a song by
TASMANIA (who often provide music for this

00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:03.349
channel).

00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:06.240
But let’s assume I upload their song without
permission.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.590
This means I’ve created something ILLEGAL.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:13.530
TASMANIA, or the record label who holds their
rights, they can sue ME…

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:18.129
Bad news for me.

00:05:18.129 --> 00:05:20.240
They can request a big fine from me.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.669
So do they do it?

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:26.460
Well… probably not, I’m a small creator
and my pockets are just not that deep.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.479
So, what can TASMANIA do?

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:32.110
Well… they can go to YouTube and they ask
them to take down the video.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:37.330
Indeed, this process is often mostly automatic:
YouTube actually has a tool, a smart filter,

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.530
called CONTENT ID.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.020
In this case, Tasmania would send all the
information about their song to YouTube.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.139
This way YouTube can scan the platform looking
for the song.

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:49.379
Anytime they find it, they can take down the
offending video.

00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:52.530
This is the law works.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:06:01.689
All fine, but hold on just a second because
this is where the interesting part comes in!

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:04.039
Now Tasmania comes in and they ask YouTube:

00:06:04.039 --> 00:06:05.039
‘Hey YouTube, errrrr.

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:09.560
How about instead of just taking this down
you slice me off a bit of that sweet ad revenue

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.639
pie and send it my way?!”

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:15.800
So, according to the letter of the law, YouTube
has to block the song if requested.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:20.569
But the law doesn’t say anything about paying
licences (which is essentially what Tasmania

00:06:20.569 --> 00:06:21.569
want).

00:06:21.569 --> 00:06:23.009
Nonetheless… since YouTube is pretty chill.

00:06:23.009 --> 00:06:25.240
they wanna do business with Tasmania!

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.360
So they essentially say:

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.599
“Alright then, look, we can negotiate a
licence… and we’ll give you a little slice.

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:31.599
Sounds cool?”

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:35.910
In other words: they negotiate a CONTRACT.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.470
And now you might wonder… well how big is
that slice of the ad revenue pie.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.740
Well… according to the record labels, it
is pretty low.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.470
Just look at what Paul McCartney has said:

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:52.229
https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/sir-paul-mccartney-tells-european-parliament-vote-through-article-13-to-ensure-a-sustainable-future-for-music/
…today some User Upload Content platforms

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:57.099
refuse to compensate artists and all music
creators fairly for their work, while they

00:06:57.099 --> 00:06:59.620
exploit it for their own profit.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.659
–Sir Paul McCartney

00:07:01.659 --> 00:07:03.120
The question now is…

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.780
Is Paul McCartney right or wrong?

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.439
YouTube states they give the majority of the
revenue to the record label.

00:07:09.439 --> 00:07:13.500
But we don’t really know because the labels
want to keep this information confidential.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.290
We also don’t know because we asked them.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.580
And nobody would tell us.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.479
There is only one thing we know.

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:25.550
The European music associations like IFPY
or IMPALA want YouTube to pay more.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:30.990
They claim there is a so called VALUE GAP
between the revenue YouTube generates and

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.110
what they pay to them.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.669
In other words… they wanna have a stronger
negotiation position.

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:37.479
And how can they do that?

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:42.490
Well, that would be with a new copyright law
that forces platforms like YouTube to pay

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:43.580
a licence.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:51.330
This is why, in the year 2016, the European
Commission wrote the first draft for the…

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:57.360
DIRECTIVE ON COPYRIGHT IN THE DIGITAL SINGLE
MARKET

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.229
According to this law, YouTube and the rest
of the user generated content platforms must

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:03.590
compensate fairly to creators.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.630
This means YouTube doesn’t give that ad
percentage because they’re cool with it

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.789
but because they are legally compelled to.

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:10.440
And this is a big deal.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:15.680
Because this means a better negotiating position
for the record labels.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.120
Alright, so far this doesn’t sound THAT
bad, right?

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:31.099
Well… hold on a minute because now is when
things start getting more complicated...

00:08:31.099 --> 00:08:32.529
DEVIL IS IN THE DETAILS

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:36.320
As you might know, the European Commission
itself cannot pass a law.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.899
They have to send it first to the European
Parliament and to the European Council.

00:08:40.899 --> 00:08:44.600
These two institutions have to write their
own version of the law.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:50.800
And this is when the real issue with Article
13 comes up…

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:58.230
“Online content sharing service providers
perform an act of Communication to the public”

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.230
Article 13.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:00.570
Paragraph 1.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:01.700
EU Parliament version

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.390
And now you might say…

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.220
Alright, Simon, what’s up?

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:06.960
Where exactly is the problem??

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.050
Well, let me translate it for you from EU
Parliament language.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:18.079
It basically means YouTube would have unlimited
liability on the content that stays on the

00:09:18.079 --> 00:09:19.079
platform.

00:09:19.079 --> 00:09:21.730
To illustrate the impact of this, let’s
go back to the TASMANIA band example.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.110
Now, they wouldn’t sue only me.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:25.970
Now they could sue me AND YouTube!

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.790
And remember my mention of deep pockets.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:30.820
YouTube has those…

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:31.820
Boy does it…

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:40.540
In other words, according to this legislation,
YouTube would have to spend millions in lawsuits.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:42.079
Just think about it.

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:49.740
Every single minute, over 300 hours of videos
are uploaded.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:54.500
Chances are many of those videos can have
some copyright violation...

00:09:54.500 --> 00:10:00.220
And you just need ONE video with a copyright
issue to give YouTube a multimillion dollar

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:01.220
headache.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:06.930
So YouTube cant deal with that kinda legal
exposure, so how would they avoid all of it?

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:10.850
Well… they would have to… build a wall.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:13.120
A very strict firewall in this case.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:16.540
Basically, any video with third party content
would have to be blocked.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:20.959
Of course, if you are in America, you could
still watch it because this law only affects

00:10:20.959 --> 00:10:23.079
the content watched or uploaded inside Europe.

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:29.490
So, if this law gets passed, good luck trying
to watch videos inside of European Union.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:36.620
And yes, I’m talking about most videos...

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:41.639
Because it’s really hard to work out what
is, and what is not, third party content.

00:10:41.639 --> 00:10:44.190
It’s almost impossible to identify it.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:48.160
Basically, everything beyond a dude talking
to a camera with a white background would

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.540
have a risk of being blocked.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.850
And that includes this video.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:56.370
Because right now I’m gonna show you a quote
from Donald Trump on CNN.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:11:06.790
Yes, I know what you are going to say now.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:11.110
Come on, Simon, you haven’t done anything
illegal there - that’s gotta be fair use,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:12.110
right?

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:13.820
Well yeah, you’re right.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:18.440
According to the law, I can use content like
this for satire or illustration.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:19.440
But guess what!

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:21.801
That YouTube firewall it’s not a lawyer,
its an algorithm.

00:11:21.801 --> 00:11:25.420
And algorithms don’t understand context
or the law.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:29.199
So this brand new YouTube firewall would just
say ‘THIRD PARTY CONTENT!

00:11:29.199 --> 00:11:30.410
IT HAS TO BE BLOCKED!!!!’

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:37.100
And the same goes when I show you this clip
from TASMANIA:

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:42.160
Is this illegal?

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:43.449
Of course not!

00:11:43.449 --> 00:11:46.880
But good luck making a robot understand legal
caveats.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.649
And really, all my YouTube comrades watching
will attest to this, you already know how

00:11:52.649 --> 00:11:55.390
many problems we have with the filters YouTube
already have.

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.860
So imagine how tough it will be with an even
more aggressive firewall.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.870
But HOLD ON just a minute because there is
an alternative!

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.740
There is indeed a solution.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:09.620
A solution that makes everybody moderately
happy and that is pretty realistic....

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:16.050
WHAT CAN IT BE DONE ABOUT THIS?

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.340
As I told you before, this copyright directive
has been voted on in the European Parliament.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.770
But this doesn’t mean the legislation has
been passed!

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:25.600
In fact, we’re still in the middle of it
all.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.519
You see, the process to pass an EU directive
it goes as follows.

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:32.780
First step, the Commission creates a first
draft.

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.990
That’s how we got the first Article 13 draft.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:41.360
Second step, this draft goes to the EU Parliament,
they write another draft and vote on it.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.459
This is why we have another version of Article
13.

00:12:44.459 --> 00:12:54.170
But now there is a third step and a third
player coming in: at thats the European Council.

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:57.320
The European Council is where all the member
states are represented.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.570
And, oh yes, they have approved ANOTHER version
of the Article 13.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:04.440
So yes, my dear viewers… we don’t have
just one Article 13.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:10.279
We have three different versions: from the
commission, parliament and council.

00:13:10.279 --> 00:13:13.440
European political institutions are so simple…

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.589
Said no one.

00:13:14.589 --> 00:13:15.589
Ever.

00:13:15.589 --> 00:13:16.589
So what happens next?

00:13:16.589 --> 00:13:20.949
Well, now we are in the third step, which
is when the Commission, Parliament and Council

00:13:20.949 --> 00:13:22.690
get together and negotiate.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:29.009
In Brussels speak, we would call this the
TRIALOGUES.

00:13:29.009 --> 00:13:34.580
(Yes, sort of a DIALOGUE, but with three people…

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:39.279
Like I said, never simple in Brussels, even
the damn words they use).

00:13:39.279 --> 00:13:42.019
So imagine a big room with a big round table.

00:13:42.019 --> 00:13:45.170
On one hand, we have the Commission representative
and her advisors.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:50.260
On the other, the representative of the Parliament…
and on the other, the one from the Council.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:54.550
Counting all the representatives and the advisors,
we have over 30 people negotiating.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.339
They meet almost every week.

00:13:56.339 --> 00:14:01.080
And yes, this is a loooooong process.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:02.230
Shocker.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:03.769
In this case, we have 3 different texts.

00:14:03.769 --> 00:14:09.610
And believe me, the one from the council is
very different.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:17.840
“In the absence of the authorisation (…) an
online content sharing provider shall not

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:22.810
be liable for the act of the communication
to the public (…) when: it demonstrates

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:29.050
that has made best efforts (…) and upon
notification by rightholders (…) it has

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:35.600
acted expeditiously to remove or disable access
to these works”

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:36.600
Article 13.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:37.720
Paragraph 4.

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:39.769
OK, let me translate it to you again.

00:14:39.769 --> 00:14:43.769
This means that YouTube is liable for the
content on their platform.

00:14:43.769 --> 00:14:46.440
But this liability is mitigated.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:49.000
Alright, so what does this mean?

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.839
OK, let’s go back to the TASMANIA example.

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:55.680
With this law in hand, Youtube must report
to them the songs uploaded on the platform.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:59.750
And they have to ask them whether they want
to take them down or to get a licence from

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:00.750
it.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:05.050
At this moment, Tasmania will have to add
their data and metadata into a form.

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:09.970
This way, that filter YouTube already has,
the so called CONTENT ID system, already knows

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:11.000
what to track.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.220
But not only that!

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:16.350
According to this Article 13 version, if YouTube
shows they have done everything possible to

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:20.699
protect the copyright, they cannot be sued.

00:15:20.699 --> 00:15:29.950
In other words, if YouTube plays by the rules,
they can avoid a lot of legal uncertainty.

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.910
Yes, they would have to pay more money to
the record labels.

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:35.339
But there is no existential risk anymore.

00:15:35.339 --> 00:15:39.550
And of course, we, the content creators, would
be happy too because we wouldn’t have to

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:41.889
worry about all of our videos being blocked.

00:15:41.889 --> 00:15:46.910
And finally, the record labels are also happy
because they get what they need: better conditions

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:49.089
for negotiating.

00:15:49.089 --> 00:15:56.070
So let me summarize it for you: what’s the
difference between the Parliament and the

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:57.070
Council positions?

00:15:57.070 --> 00:16:01.600
The consequences of the Parliament’s version
is that European YouTube would become just

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:02.600
another TV channel.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:05.250
Only the creators who already have a big audience
would survive.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:06.250
Why?

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.990
Because they are the only ones who can pay
a lawyer to manage all the copyright issues

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:11.570
so YouTube will let them upload videos.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:17.569
But there would be a big wall, pretty much
impossible to surpass, for the hundred of

00:16:17.569 --> 00:16:19.730
thousands of small creators.

00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:23.870
But there is a way to find a common ground
for all the players here.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:28.750
Of course, it is a compromise, but that’s
what politics are all about.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:32.050
So now, the big question is… what comes
after this third step?

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:33.390
What’s the fourth step?

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:38.350
Well, after this legislation has been negotiated
in the trialogues, they bring it back to the

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:39.350
parliament.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:40.940
There they vote yes or no to the text.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.100
And then we go to the final step.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:44.779
This is just a EU directive.

00:16:44.779 --> 00:16:49.139
A EU directive is not an actual law but rather
a guideline.

00:16:49.139 --> 00:16:53.089
So after the directive gets passed, it has
to go to all the member states where they

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:54.389
turn it into laws.

00:16:54.389 --> 00:16:58.829
And this is another moment were online creators
have to show up and speak to their national

00:16:58.829 --> 00:16:59.829
governments.

00:16:59.829 --> 00:17:03.680
And yes, if you’re a European YouTube creator
watching this, right now… that includes

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.680
you.

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:08.360
If there is something we have learned from
our trip to Brussels it’s that policy makers

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:09.730
don’t even know we exist.

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:14.050
And, if they don’t know us, as an industry,
if they don’t know that thousands of people

00:17:14.050 --> 00:17:18.480
live off of creating online content… they
will not understand how many people might

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:21.319
go unemployed because of a bad regulation.

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:25.010
So if you liked this video, rather than ask
you to subscribe today, I’d suggest you

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.909
share it with a friend - a creator, a fan,
a European.

00:17:27.909 --> 00:17:30.049
Hopefully someone who will help make a difference.

00:17:30.049 --> 00:17:34.330
At some point in the future, we’ll probably
all need to act on this, and here at Visual

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:40.230
Politik, we’ll keep you posted.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:45.270
Thanks for watching.

